{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/107527-storytellers-only-dreams-of-avaris","id":107527,"title":"(Storytellers Only) Dreams of Avaris","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-04-07T10:26:29-07:00","id":107527,"title":"(Storytellers Only) Dreams of Avaris","content":"I always wondered why that particular word was used in the book: avarice. While, certainly, it smacks of a certain irony (what Judges call selfish greed, the heretics see as precious freedom). Certainly, it's a bold declaration. But what if there is more to it? Avaris was an egyptian city, on the eastern side of the Nile Delta. Excavations dont showed that Avaris was once the capitol of Egypt, during the reign of the Hyksos. They found evidence of strong trade. There was a port. There were frescoes in Minoan style. They even found that part of the Nile had been partially controled and guided through the city. While, sure.. all the evidence points to a Hyksos era period of Avaris's height.. what if there is more? What if Avaris was built on the former site of the City of Pillars? What if, when Arisen dream of the City of Pillars, they are dreaming of Avaris?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","name":"Cleverest of Things","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar935_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"650"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-07T10:30:29-07:00","id":107531,"title":"","content":"Oh I always thought it was Dreams of Avarice because it was a journal on Apotheosis, because the Arisen Arise as Slave but to achieve Apotheosis they can finally live for themselves. I wonder if the Heretic was a fan of Ayn Rand.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-07T10:33:34-07:00","id":107534,"title":"","content":"That is the literal reading, but again, why Avarice and not Freedom? just to be facetious? I didn't think much of it (and had used the whole &quot;judges consider freedom=avarice&quot;) until I stumbled upon the city Avaris. Reading it was a trade hub and capitol that had the same far-reaching connections as Irem allgedly has, I wondered if there was more to it. That maybe the Heretic was trying to leave subtle clues.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","name":"Cleverest of Things","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar935_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"650"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-07T11:21:05-07:00","id":107566,"title":"","content":"Weirdly I took a whole other meaning from the title. <br /> <br /> Dreams of Avarice is a book written by the Heretic to be a sorta manifesto on Apotheosis. So considering that the title could as Eldagusto mentioned refer to the &quot;greed&quot; of an Arisen following the path to Apotheosis. His/Her desire for freedom being a &quot;greed&quot; and a dereliction of the holy duty the Arisen have to the judges.<br /> <br /> I thought it might also be a sorta &quot;hell all&quot; article or piece about the Truth of what was going on with Irem and the Shan'Iatu. Namely about their greed for power and immortality/godhood at any cost. Might even carry though the idea to the modern day with the &quot;Greed&quot; of the Judges constantly demanding Sekhem and the endless service of he Arisen. <br /> <br /> After all, any dogma bound Arisen the read such a thing would see it as Heretical. So this might be the root of the Heretics name among other Arisen. <br /> <br /> It could also be about Ammut, its heavily implied after all that the Shan'Iatu worshiped her as the &quot;one true god&quot; kinda. We know they at least made a deal with her and had did not have the power to weasel out of it. She wishes to devour pretty much everything so her &quot;greed&quot; is pretty endless. Maybe it will talk about the Shan'Iatu's greed in relation to hers. <br /> <br /> Then again maybe it will talk about all these things in different sections. Since its compared to Book of Nod in its style its likely to be divided into chapters. Much like one chapter of the Book of Nod is about Caine becoming a Vampire and another is about the actions of his childer and grand childer I imagine Dreams will have a section dedicated to the Heretics own backstory and another dedicated to telling the &quot;truth&quot; of Irem.<br /> <br /> Will it have anything to do with the Egyptian city of Avarice? Don't know...maybe? Guess we'll wait and see. I ask CAS a few questions about the book on the preview thread, so hopefully we'll get some more info soon.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#0000cd\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>&quot;There is no God in the Badlands.&quot; </i></span></span></span><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">- Sunny</span></span><br /> <br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img41/8167/obcp.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/599-hawthorn","name":"Hawthorn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar599_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"511"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-07T13:54:03-07:00","id":107661,"title":"","content":"All of those are clearly the more obvious, and story impactful meanings of the title, yes. But they are the obvious ones, and while true, may not be the only meaning. It's possible for things to have a subtle, second meaning (perhaps playing Demon has gotten ciphers and such into my head). I just find it odd that Avaris is a greek name for an egyptian capitol, and Avarice is the greed of either/both the arisen and the shan-iatu. Of course, I prefer the Al Fayyum area to the Nile Delta, to be a bit more exotic. It's also possible the location of the city of the pillars will be storyteller fiat rather than set the way other parts of the setting are.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","name":"Cleverest of Things","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar935_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"650"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}